  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds
of works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".  "Licensees" and
"recipients" refer to anyone who receives a copy of The Program.

  "Modify" means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work in a
fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

  "Covered Work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

  "Propagate" means to do anything with it that, without permission,
would make you directly or indirectly liable for copyright infringement,
except executing it on a computer or modifying it in private.  Propagation
includes copying, distribution (with or without modification), making
available to the public, and in some cases other operations as well.

  "Convey" means any kind of propagation that enables other parties to
make copies.  Simple interaction with a user through a computer network
does not constitute conveying.

  "Source Code" means the preferred form of the work for making
modifications to it.  "Object Code" means any non-source form of a work.

  "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular product or service, an interface
widely used by developers working in that field.

  "System Libraries" of an executable work means anything, other than
the work as a whole, that (a) is included in the normal form of
packaging a User Product, and (b) is intended to implement or make
available a standard interface for which implementation is available
to the public in the form of a Standard Interface, or is intended to
implement or make available a Standard Interface, and is intended to
implement or make available a standard interface for which implementation
is available to the public in the form of a Standard Interface.

  "Corresponding Source" for a work in Object Code form means all the
source code needed to generate, install, and run the object code and to
modify the work, including scripts to control those installation and
operations.  However, it does not include the work's System Libraries.

  1. Source Code.

  The Corresponding Source for a work in Object Code form means all
the source code needed to generate, install, and (if the work is
modifiable) modify the work, including scripts to control those
installation and operations.  It does not include the work's System
Libraries.

